        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Ugh, this is so bad. Hard-coding the value in the documentation like that, especially with how we recently changed it, is bad: it's not very robust for the users of the API since we might change it (we already did, and someone might have relied on it being exactly 200), nor is it robust for us - we forgot to update the documentation.\r\n\r\nYep.\r\n\r\n>     1. just drop the whole \"If no call is active at the moment, this function returns X.\" line i.e. not make a specific value promise, just have the API user treat the function as a black box, always putting its return value directly into the sleep() call, trusting toxcore to do a sensible thing.\r\n> \r\n>     2. or if someone really needs to know when there is no call, e.g. to sleep longer on mobile platforms to save on battery:\r\n>        \r\n>        1. make it a proper special value, like `0` or `UINT32_MAX` to mean \"we are not in a call\", and have the user explicitly handle it appropriately instead of passing directly into the sleep() call.\r\n\r\nZero maybe, but not `UINT32_MAX`.\r\n\r\n>        2. or make the `*_iteration_interval()` functions take an extra argument - the value the user wants toxcore to return when there is no call. `0` could be a special value for that argument for when the user wants toxcore to provide a sensible value instead, like what toxcore would do in (1). If you think that specifying `0` special value is ugly, we could even get rid of it by providing two variants of the same function - one without the extra argument and one with it, but C doesn't support function overloads so they would need to be named differently.\r\n\r\n> (1) is non-customizable, and the special return value of (2.i) is foot-gun'ish if the API user doesn't read the doc and plugs it directly into the sleep() call, (2.ii) seems to be safe from that issue.\r\n\r\nHm, this goes into the right direction. Or we could put it into some setting. But the call site max/min/clamp can already be archived.\r\nI do something like that already and `tox_iteration_interval()` needs a similar treatment. I currently take the returned value by a power to get higher interval timings...\r\n`std::pow(tc.toxIterationInterval(), 1.6f)/1000.f`\r\n\r\n",
